  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few strong to severe thunderstorms are\npossible from Central Virginia to Southern Maryland this \nafternoon.\n\nSurface observations and visible satellite show surface \nconvergence becoming maximized from Central Virginia to \nSouthern Maryland as a cold front overtakes a pre-frontal trough\nthat was already in place. Thus far, showers have struggled to \nmature, with very few, if any, producing lightning. This is \nlikely due to ample dry air present in the mid-upper levels \nevident on both the 12z IAD sounding and model soundings. As\nlow-level convergence continues to increase from Central\nVirginia to Southern Maryland, it may eventually be enough to\novercome the dry air and sustain the development of thunderstorms.\nIf a more robust storm were to develop, there could be a\nconditional threat for it to become severe, with damaging winds\nbeing the primary threat. Parameter-wise the environment is\nimpressive, with around 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE, 50-60 knots of\neffective bulk shear, and DCAPE over 1000 J/kg. However, this\ncould be a case where the high DCAPE, highly sheared environment\nis also proving unfavorable for maintaining deeper updrafts. So\nthere's a conditional severe thunderstorm threat late this\nafternoon into this evening, with damaging winds being the\nprimary threat. SPC has far southern Maryland outlooked in a\nSlight (level 2 of 5) Risk for severe thunderstorms, with a\nMarginal (level 1 of 5) Risk extending a bit further north and\nwest. Any showers or storms should gradually wind down through\nthe evening, with drier conditions expected during the second\nhalf of the night. Cooler, drier air will start to filter in\nbehind the front, with overnight lows dropping back into the 50s\nto the west of the Blue Ridge, with low-mid 60s further east.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected Thursday through Saturday.\n\nA sprawling area of high pressure will build to our north for \nThursday and Friday, before shifting off the New England \nCoastline on Saturday. Northerly flow ahead of the approaching \nhigh will advect a much cooler and drier airmass into the region\nfor Thursday. Well below normal temperatures and dewpoints are \nforecast for late July, with high temperatures only in the upper\n70s/low 80s. Dewpoints in the 50s will also make it feel much \nless humid than is typical for July. Mostly sunny skies will \naccompany the well below normal temperatures/dewpoints. \nTemperatures will stay well below normal for Friday and Saturday\nwithin easterly flow. Some moisture could be drawn northward\nfrom the stalled frontal zone across the southeastern states\nduring this time. The current forecast has some low rain chances\nboth days, mainly in southern portions of the CWA and along the\nAllegheny Mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Gradual increase in moisture & precipitation \nchances early next week. \n\nBroad high pressure will continue to shift northeast over the \nweekend, with low pressure building over northeastern Canada \nand a stemming trough potentially forming over the Mid-Atlantic late \nSunday and into Monday. Dewpoints start to increase during this \ntime, so shower & thunderstorm chances could increase during the \nafternoon & evening hours Sunday and Monday as this perturbation \nmoves through. Machine-learning algorithms show some lower-end \nprobabilities for severe occurrence during this time, although \nensembles still show uncertainty in timing of the overall synoptic \nsetup. As this trough approaches, temperatures gradually begin to \nincrease back into the upper 80s.\n\nDewpoints could remain elevated within the region as this system \nmoves through, with the upper-level low to the north slowly \ntracking to the south before shifting to the northeast midweek. This \ncould overall re-introduce chances for rain within the region \nafter a brief dry spell.",
